+//! Leyline Modes: the play layer that sits beside the persistent world.
+//!
+//! World props are real realm gameobjects; mode entities (a golf ball, a course) exist only for
+//! the players in a session and are simulated on every client from the same inputs. That only
+//! works if every client computes bit-identical results, so everything here is deterministic:
+//!
+//! * fixed timestep ([`golf::DT`]), fixed iteration order (pieces in course order);
+//! * `f32` with `+ − × ÷` and `sqrt` only, which IEEE-754 rounds exactly on every target the client
+//!   runs on (wasm32, x86_64, aarch64). No `sin`/`cos`/`powf` from the platform's libm: angles
+//!   come in as integers and go through [`detmath::sin_cos_tenths`], a fixed polynomial;
+//! * inputs are integers (aim in tenths of a degree, power in permille), course text is parsed
+//!   with Rust's correctly rounded float parser.
+//!
+//! A shot's [`golf::Outcome::hash`] covers every frame of the ball's path, so two clients (or a
+//! future certifying host) can compare one number to know they saw the same shot.
+
+pub mod detmath;
+pub mod golf;
